# =============================================================================================
# Mosquitto access control list -- ACS-Cymru Asset Tracking Platform
# =============================================================================================
#
# DEFAULT IS DENY. Mosquitto grants nothing not matched by a rule below, and `allow_anonymous
# false` in mosquitto.conf means every client authenticates first.
#
# HOW `pattern` WORKS: `%u` is substituted with the connecting client's username, so a gateway
# whose MQTT username is its own `sparkplug_id` can publish only beneath its own edge-node
# segment. That is the rule doing the actual work.
#
# THERE IS NO WILDCARD-WRITE PRINCIPAL. Every account below is confined to a topic subtree it
# has a reason to reach, and no account can publish asset data for an edge node other than
# itself. This replaced a shared `factoryplus` account holding `readwrite spBv1.0/#`, which the
# ingestion daemon, the Node-RED simulator and the E2E validator all connected as -- so the
# credential that ran the demo flow could also forge DBIRTH and DDATA for every machine on the
# site, and `verify_gateway_binding()` could not tell the difference: a forged message under a
# CORRECTLY BOUND device passes that check by construction.
#
# This is the BROKER tier of a two-tier defence; the application tier is
# `verify_gateway_binding()` in ingestion/ingestion.py. Neither is sufficient alone -- the broker
# cannot know which device belongs to which gateway, and the daemon cannot stop a forged message
# reaching other subscribers.
#
# The three attacks this closes, and why a sparkplug_id is not a secret:
#   tutorial/README.md -> "Broker Topic Authorisation"
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERIFIED BEHAVIOUR OF THIS FILE, against eclipse-mosquitto:2.0.20. THE PIN IS NOW 2.0.22, and
# this note says the version it was measured on rather than following the pin, because a number
# bumped without re-running the measurement is a claim nobody made.
#
# What DOES follow the pin automatically: `scripts/check-broker-config.mjs` reads the tag straight
# out of docker-compose.yml and re-runs its assertions against whatever is pinned, so the
# confinement claims below are exercised on the current image on every run. The delivery-time
# semantics in the fourth bullet are the load-bearing part and are covered there.
#
# Bump this line only alongside a re-run of that script on the new tag.
#
#   * A gateway publishing under its OWN edge node succeeds; under ANOTHER edge node the message
#     is dropped by the broker and reaches no subscriber.
#   * The ingestion principal cannot publish DBIRTH or DDATA at all -- only NCMD.
#   * The monitoring principal can publish nothing, anywhere.
#   * SUBSCRIPTIONS CONTAINING WILDCARDS ARE NOT REFUSED. 2.0.20 grants `spBv1.0/+/NCMD/+` with
#     QoS 0 (not 128) even for a client confined by `pattern`, and enforces the ACL per message
#     at DELIVERY time instead. So the simulator's existing wildcard NCMD subscription keeps
#     working -- it still receives its own rebirth request, and does not receive another node's.
#     Narrowing those subscriptions is therefore NOT required, and was deliberately not done:
#     the topic lives in the seeded flow, which is user content, so changing it would have
#     rewritten what an operator may have edited in exchange for nothing.
#   * THE SILENT DROP CANNOT BE LIFTED BY MOVING TO MQTT 5, which was proposed for exactly that
#     and is why this is written down here rather than rediscovered. The `Not authorized` reason
#     code that would carry a refusal back to the publisher rides on PUBACK -- and there is no
#     PUBACK at QoS 0, under either protocol version. Every Sparkplug publisher on this stack is
#     QoS 0: the four `mqtt out` nodes in node_red_flow.json, the one in the physical-gateway
#     flow template, ingestion.py's rebirth NCMD, and validate.py. Raising them is not an option
#     that was left unset -- SPARKPLUG B REQUIRES QoS 0 and retain false for NBIRTH, NDATA,
#     DBIRTH, DDATA, NDEATH, DDEATH, NCMD and DCMD, delegating loss detection to the `seq`
#     counter and the rebirth request instead, both of which ingestion.py implements. So a
#     gateway publishing under the wrong edge node learns nothing from the broker by
#     construction, and DELIVERY stays the only way to assert this file.
#
# `scripts/check-broker-config.mjs` re-runs these assertions on the pinned tag. A config that
# starts is not a config that is safe.
# =============================================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-gateway confinement -- every edge node, real or simulated
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Sparkplug B topic is spBv1.0/<group_id>/<message_type>/<edge_node_id>/<device_id>, so
# `spBv1.0/+/+/%u/#` pins the FOURTH segment to the client's own username while leaving the
# group and message type free. A gateway provisioned with username == its `sparkplug_id`
# (see scripts/mosquitto-provision-gateway.mjs) therefore cannot address any other edge node.
#
# THE USERNAME IS ALWAYS A `sparkplug_id`, AND IT CANNOT BE ANYTHING ELSE. `sparkplug_id` is a
# GENERATED column -- 'gwy' plus the first 21 hex characters of the row's UUID primary key -- and
# the edge-node segment of the topic must equal it or `verify_gateway_binding()` rejects the
# message. So a friendly name like `val_gateway_01` is not merely discouraged here, it would fail
# at the application tier even if the broker allowed it.
#
# That is also what makes a pre-provisioned credential possible for something that creates its
# gateway at runtime: pin the UUID and the wire identity is known in advance. Every gateway on this
# stack does exactly that -- `Sim_Gateway_Cell1_Machining` (12000000-…-0001 ->
# gwy120000000000400080000, seeded by 0002_seed_data.sql and provisioned along with the other three
# cells, now retired) and the validator's gateway (11000000-…-0001 ->
# gwy110000000000400080000, upserted by ingestion/validate.py).
#
# `gwy100000000000400080000` is still in the password file and is NOT in this list on purpose: it
# belonged to the retired single-device simulator, whose gateway row 0020 deletes. It needs no rule
# of its own -- the `%u` pattern above already confines it -- and it is kept only so a pre-existing
# Node-RED volume can still connect. Nothing on a current stack publishes under it.
#
# Read is granted on the same subtree so a gateway can receive its own Sparkplug command
# topics (NCMD/DCMD) -- and, deliberately, on nothing else: one gateway has no business
# subscribing to another's telemetry.
pattern readwrite spBv1.0/+/+/%u/#

# Sparkplug primary-host STATE topics are a broadcast birth/death for host applications. Every
# edge node needs to read them to know whether the primary host is online; none may write one.
#
# Declared as a global `topic` rule, not a `pattern`: it is the same subtree for every client,
# and Mosquitto warns at startup about a `pattern` carrying no %u/%c substitution -- correctly,
# since such a pattern is just a global rule written misleadingly.
topic read spBv1.0/STATE/#


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ingestion daemon -- a consumer, plus exactly one command
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The daemon subscribes to the whole `spBv1.0/#` tree; that is its job. It is otherwise NOT a
# publisher of asset data, and this split is what says so in a way the broker enforces.
#
# Its one write INSIDE SPARKPLUG is the rebirth request: when it meets a DDATA metric carrying an
# alias it cannot resolve (its alias table is in memory and empty after a restart) it publishes
# `Node Control/Rebirth` to spBv1.0/<group>/NCMD/<edge_node>.
#
# `spBv1.0/+/NCMD/+` and not `.../#`: an NCMD topic has exactly four segments, so the trailing
# `#` would grant a subtree that does not exist.
user factoryplus_ingestion
topic read  spBv1.0/#
topic write spBv1.0/+/NCMD/+
# The Directory's MQTT half (ingestion/directory_publish.py). OUTSIDE `spBv1.0/` deliberately: a
# Directory document is not a Sparkplug message and must not arrive looking like one, and a
# separate root means the broker's default-deny covers it until a rule says otherwise instead of
# the per-gateway pattern above reaching it by accident.
#
# THE DAEMON IS THE ONLY WRITER, and this is what says so. The publisher does not consume births to
# build a registry -- it projects the enrolment records, on a timer -- so nothing else on this stack
# has any reason to write here, and a second writer would be a second answer to the same question.
topic write ACS-Cymru/Directory/#
# And reads it back, so the daemon can verify its own retained documents without a second
# account. See "The Directory topic, and who may read it" below for why that list is short.
topic read  ACS-Cymru/Directory/#
# The Unified Namespace (ingestion/uns_publish.py): every decoded reading, retained, under
# uns/<enterprise>/<site>/<area>/<cell>/<device>/<metric>. The daemon is the only writer, and the
# tree is off by default (UNS_MQTT_ENABLED). READ IS GRANTED TO NO GATEWAY AND TO NO OTHER
# PRINCIPAL HERE: `uns/#` is every machine's readings in the clear, so one gateway credential
# reading it would read the whole plant, which the per-node pattern above exists to prevent. A
# BI or SCADA consumer gets its own account and a `topic read uns/#` rule, added deliberately.
topic write uns/#
topic read  uns/#


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Directory topic, and who may read it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS SUBTREE IS THE WHOLE ADDRESS SPACE IN ONE DOCUMENT, so read access to it is an exposure
# decision rather than a convenience. Over HTTP the Directory reads AS THE CALLER, under that
# caller's RLS policies, precisely so that a live read across every asset does not become a way
# around them. A topic has no caller: every subscriber the broker admits reads the same bytes.
#
# SO READ IS GRANTED ONLY TO THE TWO PRINCIPALS THAT ALREADY HOLD `spBv1.0/#`, and the grants sit
# in each one's own block above and below rather than in a block of their own here. Both of them already
# see the entire fleet's traffic, so the Directory tells them nothing they could not observe -- the
# grant widens no principal's view. Neither of them subscribes here today; the rules exist so that
# turning the publisher on does not also require an ACL edit, and so that the DEFAULT set of readers
# is written down rather than implied.
#
# NO GATEWAY IS IN THIS LIST, AND THAT IS THE POINT. A gateway is confined to its own edge node's
# subtree so it cannot enumerate the site. Granting it the Directory would undo that in one line and
# would be invisible -- reading is silent, and nothing downstream would ever report it.
#
# A deployment wanting a headless Directory consumer adds its account and a rule here, deliberately.
# The publisher is off by default (DIRECTORY_MQTT_ENABLED), so on a stack that has not made that
# decision this subtree is empty and these rules grant access to nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# i3X server -- read only, no write of any kind
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It holds its own `spBv1.0/#` subscription because i3X subscription values must accumulate
# between client polls and cannot come from Supabase Realtime (`telemetry` is a postgres_fdw
# foreign table whose rows never enter Supabase's WAL). It publishes NOTHING: `PUT /objects/value`
# answers 405 and `/info` declares `update.current: false`.
#
# SEPARATE FROM THE INGESTION ACCOUNT PRECISELY BECAUSE OF THAT. Sharing one would give a service
# that refuses writes by design a credential that can publish NCMD, and the durable control on
# this service is that it cannot be talked into writing -- an argument the broker should be able
# to make too, not just the code.
user factoryplus_i3x
topic read spBv1.0/#
# The Directory, under the section above: i3X already sees the whole fleet's traffic, so this
# grant widens nothing. It does not subscribe here today.
topic read ACS-Cymru/Directory/#


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Broker monitoring -- $SYS only
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `#` does not match topics beginning with `$`, so the broker-status tree needs naming
# explicitly and cannot be reached by any of the rules above.
#
# Two consumers, both read-only and neither of which touches Sparkplug at all: the mosquitto
# healthcheck in docker-compose.yml (and the chart's liveness/readiness probes), which subscribe
# to `$SYS/broker/version`; and the Prometheus exporter sidecar on Kubernetes, which re-serves
# `$SYS/broker/#` as `broker_*` metrics.
#
# THIS ACCOUNT EXISTS BECAUSE DELETING THE SHARED ONE WOULD OTHERWISE TAKE THE STACK DOWN, not as
# tidiness: without it the healthcheck never succeeds, the broker never reports healthy, and
# every service that depends on it fails to start -- a total outage whose cause names neither
# MQTT nor this file.
user factoryplus_monitor
topic read $SYS/#
